{
  "name": "How to add schema markup to your site effectively",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1: Identify page types and Google-supported features",
      "text": "Before you dive into coding, take a look at your website's pages. What kind of content is on them? Are they product pages, articles, FAQs, local business profiles? Then, research which schema.org types Google actually supports for rich results and enhanced features. Google recommends JSON-LD, so aim for that where you can. Knowing what's possible upfront saves a lot of rework.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 2: Add markup that accurately reflects visible content",
      "text": "This is crucial: your schema markup *must* match what's visible to users on your page. Don't try to hide keywords or mark up information that isn't actually there. If you're using `Article` schema, make sure the `headline` and `author` properties directly correspond to the article's title and listed author. The goal is clarity and accuracy, providing AI with an unambiguous understanding of your content.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 3: Validate your structured data thoroughly",
      "text": "After adding the markup, use Google's Rich Results Test to check for errors and warnings specific to rich result eligibility. For broader schema.org validation, the Schema Markup Validator is your friend. This step is non-negotiable! It catches common mistakes like missing required properties or incorrect data types before they cause issues on your live site.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 4: Deploy to a limited set of pages and monitor",
      "text": "Don't roll out new schema sitewide without testing. Apply it to a representative sample of pages first. Once deployed, use the URL Inspection tool in Google Search Console to confirm that Google can access and correctly interpret your new markup. Keep an eye on your Search Console rich result status reports and performance data over time. This helps you catch any issues that might pop up due to template changes or CMS updates.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 5: Continuously monitor and update",
      "text": "Schema markup isn't a set-it-and-forget-it task. Search engine guidelines and your website content can change. Regularly recheck your reports in Search Console. Any time you have a major site redesign, a CMS update, or new features, it’s a good idea to re-validate. If you're seeing recurring errors or if your site has complex dynamic content, bringing in a pro can be a game-changer. They can help navigate the complexities and ensure your schema strategy is always on point, boosting your eligibility for those valuable AI citations.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 5
    }
  ],
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "speakable": {
    "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
    "cssSelector": [
      "h1",
      ".howto-step",
      "[itemprop=\"step\"]"
    ]
  },
